{
  "//": "Type-checks test files. Run as the second half of `pnpm typecheck`.",
  "//why": "Nothing type-checked a test file until this existed. `tsconfig.typecheck.json` reaches only `packages/*/src`, and excludes test files on top of that — so a test could import a type that does not exist and stay green forever, because vitest's esbuild transform strips types without checking them. That is exactly what happened: admin-types' own test kept importing `type AdminCollectionConfig` for the whole refactor, after the type had been deliberately deleted, and the first thing to notice was a Docker build in an end-to-end run.",
  "//scope": "Only the packages listed below, because what is left out carries 1617 pre-existing errors between six packages: server-postgres (1037), client (265), server (165), admin's remaining tests (87), the rest of app/test (52) and cli (11 — the closest to earning its line). Those are real debt; fixing a package's test types is what earns it a line here, and every line below names the drift that adding it exposed. Counts are per package measured on its own, `test` and any `src`-side `*.test.ts` together. `packages/types/__tests__` is deliberately absent and should stay absent: the `bivariance/` files are probes that assert generics are *not* assignable, so they are supposed to error.",
  "extends": "./tsconfig.typecheck.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    // The parent pins `types: ["node"]`, which leaves `describe`/`it`/`expect`
    // undeclared. In the whole-monorepo program they happened to resolve anyway,
    // because some other package pulled `@types/jest` in and global types leak
    // across a program — not something to rely on. cms-types runs on jest; a
    // vitest package added to `include` would want `"vitest/globals"` here too.
    "types": [
      "node",
      "jest"
    ]
  },
  "include": [
    "packages/cms-types/test",
    // `augment.ts` only declares `admin` if it is *in the program*, and the test
    // imports `../src/admin_collection` directly, not the package index that
    // side-effect-imports it. Without this line every `admin` in the fixtures is
    // an error — the same program-scoping rule the templates deal with via
    // `config/cms.d.ts`.
    "packages/cms-types/src",
    // The engine gates: which property primitives and which collection fields
    // each driver admits. Every assertion there is a `@ts-expect-error`, so the
    // file is inert unless tsc reads it — and for as long as it was not listed
    // here it asserted against `driver:`, `collectionPath` and a `StringProperty`
    // shape that had all been gone for months, passing every run.
    "packages/types/test",
    "packages/types/src",
    // The driver's own fixtures. `buildSearchConditions` read `prop.dataType`,
    // a field no property in `@rebasepro/types` has ever had, so search matched
    // no field on any real collection and every query fell through to `$text` —
    // which throws `IndexNotFound` without a text index. The fixtures were
    // written with the same wrong key, so the test data agreed with the bug.
    // They are real `CollectionConfig` properties now, and this line is what
    // keeps them that way.
    "packages/server-mongo/test",
    // The hook cast its returned object to `PostgresDataDriver`, which hid an
    // undeclared `name` and the fact that `admin` — and every capability on it
    // — is optional. The cast is gone; this earns the test the real `SaveProps`
    // and `DeleteProps` shapes, in place of the ones it was inventing.
    // `InsightsCache.test.ts` lives *under* `src`, so the package's `src` is
    // what has to be listed. It built `InsightDataResult` fixtures of
    // `{ data, columns }` — a shape the type has not had since it became
    // `{ rows }`, and that no production code still produces.
    "packages/plugin-insights/src",
    // `useDataEnhancementPlugin.test.tsx` indexed `plugin.slots[0]` and
    // `plugin.providers[0]`, both optional on `Plugin`. An
    // `expect(...).toBeDefined()` on the line above narrows nothing for tsc, so
    // the indexed reads only compiled because no one was checking. They are
    // `toMatchObject` shapes now, which need no narrowing.
    "packages/plugin-ai/src",
    // `useLiveRls.test.tsx` built its `executeSql` stub with a bare `jest.fn()`,
    // which infers a non-promise return and so collapses every
    // `mockResolvedValue` argument to `never` — the rows this hook parses were
    // never checked against the shape it parses them into.
    "packages/studio/test",
    // `parseSpec.test.ts` sits *under* `src`, so `src` is what has to be listed.
    // Its `makeSpec` builder was annotated `: OpenApiSpec` while supplying
    // neither of the two fields `OpenApiSpec` requires, and its parameter
    // fixture widened `in: "path"` to `string` — so a suite about parsing
    // OpenAPI documents was parsing things that are not OpenAPI documents.
    "packages/studio/src",
    // `utils.test.ts` read `result.a.b.c` off a `setIn` that returns `unknown`
    // by contract (its return shape is not its argument's). Those assertions
    // compare whole objects now, which is both cast-free and stricter.
    "packages/forms/test",
    // `components.test.tsx` rendered `<Alert severity="error">` — a prop
    // `AlertProps` does not declare, silently dropped by React, so a test named
    // "renders alert with correct message" was passing against a default-blue
    // info alert. And `views.test.tsx` wrote `VirtualTable<any>`, which tsc
    // rejects: `React.memo` had pinned the component's type parameter, so the
    // exported component was not generic at all.
    "packages/ui/test",
    // `src` joins `test` for the one `.test.tsx` that lives beside its code.
    "packages/ui/src",
    // The remaining directories that were already clean and cost nothing but a
    // line each. `codegen` and `mcp` had never been read by any tsc program;
    // `utils` is imported by everything, so its fixtures drifting is the kind
    // of thing that surfaces four packages away.
    "packages/codegen/test",
    "packages/mcp/test",
    "packages/utils/test",
    // `rls-check` ships to strangers' databases and had no type check at all —
    // no `paths` entry, no `include` line, and a package-local `typecheck`
    // script no CI job invokes. `src` joins `test` because most of its tests
    // sit beside the code.
    "packages/rls-check/test",
    "packages/rls-check/src",
    // `util.test.ts` built an "EnumValueConfig values" fixture whose `color`
    // widened to `string`, which `ColorKey | ColorScheme` does not admit — so
    // the case the test is named after was not the shape it claimed to be.
    "packages/inference/test",
    // The collection registry, permissions and policy tests.
    // `collection_registry_property_gates.test.ts` declared its fixtures with
    // `driver: "firestore"` / `driver: "mongodb"`. The field is `engine`; there
    // is no `driver`. So every "Firestore" and "MongoDB" case in it was a
    // default-Postgres collection and no engine gate was ever crossed — for as
    // long as this line was missing, nothing could say so.
    // `src` joins `test` because some of these tests sit beside the code.
    // One file, not the directory. `RebaseProps` is generic over `USER` and,
    // since the typed-SDK fix, over the database too; the assertions in
    // `rebase_client_prop_types.test.ts` are compile-time only, so they are
    // inert unless tsc reads them. The rest of `packages/app/test` carries 52
    // pre-existing errors — fixtures declaring properties with no `name` — and
    // clearing those is what would earn the directory its own line here.
    "packages/app/test/rebase_client_prop_types.type-test.ts",
    "packages/app/src",
    "packages/common/test",
    "packages/common/src",
    // `packages/firebase` declares no test runner — a recorded gap in
    // `tooling/scripts/check-test-scripts.mjs`, and the reason its tests have never
    // executed. Until one lands, tsc reading them is the only thing standing
    // between the assertions and the code they assert on.
    "packages/firebase/test",
    // Four files, not the directory. The import/export regression tests build
    // wire-shaped fixtures — a paginated `find` that clamps the way the REST
    // ingress does, an `ImportSaveError` read for the row it names — and a
    // fixture that drifts from those shapes is the whole failure mode they
    // exist to stop. The rest of `packages/cms/test` carries pre-existing
    // errors (fixtures declaring properties with no `name`), and clearing those
    // is what would earn the directory its own line here.
    // Same reasoning, one directory over: the relation picker's create-in-place
    // path is asserted through the props it hands the side panel, and a prop
    // name that drifts is exactly what the test would stop reporting.
    "packages/cms/test/components/relation-selector-create.test.tsx",
    "packages/cms/test/components/selection-dialog-create.test.tsx",
    "packages/cms/test/data_export/fetch_export_data.test.ts",
    "packages/cms/test/data_import/csv.test.ts",
    "packages/cms/test/data_import/prototype_pollution.test.ts",
    "packages/cms/test/data_import/save_entities.test.ts",
    // Files, not directories, and each one for the same reason: it carries a
    // `@ts-expect-error`, which is a test written in the type system and inert
    // in a file no program reads. `pnpm check:ts-expect-error-coverage` is what
    // keeps this half of the list from being quietly deleted to make
    // `pnpm typecheck` green.
    //
    // The five cloud suites. Four of their directives were on a
    // `process.stdout.write` shim that has type-checked for a while, so tsc
    // reports them as UNUSED the moment it reads the file — and two assertions
    // the suites are named after (`webhooks delete` never deleting the project
    // slug; the auth probe posting a JSON body) read `mock.calls[0][n]` off a
    // bare `vi.fn()`, whose calls tuple is empty, so they were tuple-index
    // errors rather than checks. The rest of `packages/cli` carries 11
    // pre-existing errors; clearing those is what earns the directory a line.
    "packages/cli/src/commands/cloud/cloud-args.test.ts",
    "packages/cli/src/commands/cloud/cloud-commands.test.ts",
    "packages/cli/src/commands/cloud/context.test.ts",
    "packages/cli/src/commands/cloud/debug.test.ts",
    "packages/cli/src/commands/cloud/emit-json-arg.test.ts",
    // `packages/client` carries 265 pre-existing errors, so one file: the
    // opt-out asserts a realtime option that is not on the type.
    "packages/client/src/realtime-optout.test.ts",
    // `packages/server` carries 165, so two files. `define-cron.test.ts`'s
    // directive sat one line above the property it meant to reject — tsc reports
    // an excess property where it is written — so "rejects misspelled fields at
    // compile time" rejected nothing. `auth-config-types.test.ts` is the guard
    // against a `[key: string]: unknown` catch-all landing on
    // `RebaseAuthConfig`, and its own docblock says "the real value is that tsc
    // validates the @ts-expect-error annotations"; nothing had.
    "packages/server/src/cron/define-cron.test.ts",
    "packages/server/test/auth-config-types.test.ts"
  ],
  "exclude": [
    "**/node_modules",
    "**/dist"
  ]
}
